   "notes": [
    [
     "income_tax",
     "reading: A filer with Schedule C or Schedule F earnings who states person-paid health insurance premiums deducts them under 26 U.S.C. 162(l): the Form 7206 instructions let a self-employed person filing Schedule C or F hold the policy in the business's name or the person's own (sources/irs/i7206.txt:59-63), so no other fact about the plan is needed, and is_self_employed is not required. See se_health_insurance for the limit."
    ],
    [
     "income_tax",
     "reading: A filer with Schedule C or Schedule F earnings deducts the person-paid premiums medical expenses count for the filer (health_insurance_premiums where stated, else the premiums without Medicare Part B) under 26 U.S.C. 162(l), up to the earned income of 401(c)(2) from the business under which the plan is taken as established, the one with the larger earned income: that business's income less its share of the deduction for half the self-employment tax (164(f)) among the filer's positive self-employment sources, and for Schedule C less the self-employed pension deduction. A stated has_esi of the filer or spouse is eligibility for an employer's subsidized plan and bars the deduction (162(l)(2)(B)); otherwise none is stated. Premiums deducted here leave medical expenses (162(l)(3)). policyengine-us deducts only health_insurance_premiums of a filer stated is_self_employed (self_employed_health_insurance_premiums.py: defined_for is_self_employed), up to self-employment income before those two reductions."
    ],
    [
     "income_tax",
     "head: the self-employed health insurance deduction is 100 under 26 U.S.C. 162(l) (earned income of 401(c)(2) from the business, 14,428); policyengine-us allows 0 (it deducts only health_insurance_premiums of a filer stated is_self_employed, up to Schedule C income)"
    ]
   ],
   "members_with_own_payroll": [],
   "itemized_notes": [
    [
     "income_tax",
     "reading: Stated other health insurance premiums are not counted again: policyengine-us documents them as part of the stated premiums without Medicare Part B (variables/household/expense/health/other_health_insurance_premiums.py), so counting both would double count. Medical expenses take the premiums without Medicare Part B."
    ],
